#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eade8768-b97c-5e90-ad02-255b9e8f8dae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "ae7ee9c8-3162-564e-a3f2-f235b60527f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13903" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2 = wz - 2w +1$ for the variable $w.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $w$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =wz-2w+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =wz-2w+1-1 \\\\ 1 & =wz-2w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $w$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =wz-2w \\\\ 1 & =w(z-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(z-2)$ to isolate $w\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =w(z-2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w(z-2)}{z-2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w\\cancel{(z-2)}}{\\cancel{z-2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{z-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $z-2\\neq 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc2b01b-a60c-53a7-837e-1c6d9a941e98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "7f0aa09d-c765-5ab5-ac89-3fb4ce168d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-13905" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4ky=5y + 1$ for the variable $y.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $5y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4ky & =5y+1 \\\\ 4ky-5y & =5y+1-5y \\\\ 4ky-5y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $y$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4ky-5y & =1 \\\\ y(4k-5) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4k -5)$ to isolate $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4k-5) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{y(4k-5)}{(4k-5)} & =\\frac{1}{(4k-5)} \\\\ \\frac{y\\cancel{(4k-5)}}{\\cancel{(4k-5)}} & =\\frac{1}{4k-5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4k-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $4k-5\\neq 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b201f4cd-b39c-5ca5-9565-0618859cd15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "f9e1e805-32a3-5d25-8b09-36707350ab61" :question/math-academy-id "e-13907" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{xy}{3} = \\dfrac{y - 1}{2}$ for the variable $y.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{3} & =\\frac{y-1}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot xy & =3\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ 2xy & =3y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =3y-3 \\\\ 2xy-3y & =3y-3-3y \\\\ 2xy-3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $y$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3y & =-3 \\\\ y(2x-3) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(2x - 3)$ to isolate $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2x-3) & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{y(2x-3)}{2x-3} & =\\frac{-3}{2x-3} \\\\ \\frac{y\\cancel{(2x-3)}}{\\cancel{2x-3}} & =\\frac{-3}{2x-3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $2x-3\\neq 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eade8768-b97c-5e90-ad02-255b9e8f8dae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "786a511d-e212-586c-b3f2-d3847309dfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10t-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487802} {:answer/id #uuid "9710bc14-6ad4-56a3-9aef-e0f30c762acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487851} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8b49ad-93a5-5255-a9f4-e73e912aa53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5t+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487856} {:answer/id #uuid "d963bb82-5c56-53d6-9bd9-cfbfe08eda09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5t-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487979} {:answer/id #uuid "e44bf7f2-164c-5379-ba4a-d9cc8c94aca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d963bb82-5c56-53d6-9bd9-cfbfe08eda09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5t-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487979} :answer-field/id #uuid "bebe6369-58e1-5cc9-8adf-5a7af1f55142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7db436d-ecf4-5342-922a-617ed93580d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-133775" :question/problem "If $5tx-3x=2,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out $x$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5tx-3x & =2 \\\\ x(5t-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(5t-3)$ to isolate $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5t-3) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x(5t-3)}{5t-3} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ \\frac{x\\cancel{(5t-3)}}{\\cancel{5t-3}} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $5t-3\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eade8768-b97c-5e90-ad02-255b9e8f8dae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579b61a3-ba63-5017-b291-f78398e84c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487743} {:answer/id #uuid "7412419a-05ad-52be-9689-89ea3aab3fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{3r+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487799} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f846ca-788b-528a-bec5-570a58cab209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3r-12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487907} {:answer/id #uuid "e114a012-2dc5-5b44-bba0-e3352f234714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3r+12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487998} {:answer/id #uuid "ffdc22e1-d675-57fc-806e-440860880dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7412419a-05ad-52be-9689-89ea3aab3fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{3r+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487799} :answer-field/id #uuid "52edd805-e394-51e3-8580-2a03d9a5fe75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b9f4cd9-9a85-5f2d-9eac-f617eac7ba39" :question/math-academy-id "q-133790" :question/problem "If $10=3rs+2s-2,$ then $s=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $s$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3rs+2s-2 \\\\ 10+2 & =3rs+2s-2+2 \\\\ 12 & =3rs+2s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $s$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =3rs+2s \\\\ 12 & =s(3r+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(3r+2)$ to isolate $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =s(3r+2) \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s(3r+2)}{3r+2} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s\\cancel{(3r+2)}}{\\cancel{3r+2}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =s \\\\ s & =\\frac{12}{3r+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3r+2\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b201f4cd-b39c-5ca5-9565-0618859cd15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e0251b-e7d9-5216-b40b-a0569fb7444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10m}{3m+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487642} {:answer/id #uuid "86b91cce-eb4e-5100-836e-cdaa979bba83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10m}{3m-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487821} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce32e74-d9b3-58fa-998a-893bfc6268a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3m}{10m-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487833} {:answer/id #uuid "d8884393-a5fe-5722-81d1-f68055378b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3m}{10m+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487978} {:answer/id #uuid "feb11450-ea29-5663-bed0-4e4bfd0d07bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3m-3}{10m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ce32e74-d9b3-58fa-998a-893bfc6268a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3m}{10m-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487833} :answer-field/id #uuid "e91e9fc2-d906-5396-a41f-f01e1ee236be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5eedbb0b-2f52-5239-8343-cadc5e74eba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-133899" :question/problem "If $\\frac{2km}{3}=\\frac{k+m}{5},$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $k$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2km}{3} & =\\frac{k+m}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2km & =3\\cdot (k+m) \\\\ 10km & =3k+3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3k$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km & =3k+3m \\\\ 10km-3k & =3k+3m-3k \\\\ 10km-3k & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $k$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $k$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $k$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km-3k & =3m \\\\ k(10m-3) & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(10m-3)$ to isolate $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(10m-3) & =3m \\\\ \\frac{k\\cancel{(10m-3)}}{\\cancel{10m-3}} & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $10m-3\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc2b01b-a60c-53a7-837e-1c6d9a941e98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6422d58b-a6aa-55ce-9272-f106d09fe53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{7p-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6422d58b-a6aa-55ce-9272-f106d09fe53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{7p-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487767} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be6f4c4-2cc2-5d19-b848-3c7a94a8c33e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03f30d76-6e4b-58e9-917f-05e941931839" :question/math-academy-id "q-215778" :question/problem "If $7pq+1=3q+8,$ then $q=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $q$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $1$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq+1 & =3q+8 \\\\ 7pq+1-1 & =3q+8-1 \\\\ 7pq & =3q+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3q$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq & =3q+7 \\\\ 7pq-3q & =3q+7-3q \\\\ 7pq-3q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $q$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $q$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $q$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq-3q & =7 \\\\ (7p-3)q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(7p-3)$ to isolate $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7p-3)q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{(7p-3)q}{7p-3} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(7p-3)}q}{\\cancel{7p-3}} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{7p-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $7p-3\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b201f4cd-b39c-5ca5-9565-0618859cd15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a03a7cf-903f-54d3-b7e5-bf17f2b1ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14n}{12-49n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a03a7cf-903f-54d3-b7e5-bf17f2b1ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14n}{12-49n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487749} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f33662-8742-5543-9baf-920fd7c6b4a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77fe19c1-2d2e-5ba1-9d49-d82b50f369df" :question/math-academy-id "q-215810" :question/problem "If $\\frac{7mn+2n}{4}=\\frac{3m}{7},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $m$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7mn+2n}{4} & =\\frac{3m}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot (7mn+2n) & =4\\cdot 3m \\\\ 49mn+14n & =12m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $49mn$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49mn+14n & =12m \\\\ 49mn+14n-49mn & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =12m-49mn\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $m$ are on the right-hand side, and all terms independent of $m$ are on the left-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $m$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =(12-49n)m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(12-49n)$ to isolate $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =(12-49n)m \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(12-49n)}m}{\\cancel{12-49n}} \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =m \\\\ m & =\\frac{14n}{12-49n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $12-49n\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc2b01b-a60c-53a7-837e-1c6d9a941e98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a8fe3c-1990-50e9-8694-e77e9c13624b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3y+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487669} {:answer/id #uuid "8533b29d-b9b1-511a-bb70-36d3cde5a702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3y-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487819} {:answer/id #uuid "a95c467d-832e-5741-85a4-f555b8d510a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4y+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487889} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c1466d-dc5c-58aa-b40b-07f2ca19557f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4y-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487971} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc8db81-fc20-5f94-9811-aaafccb7fb1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2y-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8533b29d-b9b1-511a-bb70-36d3cde5a702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3y-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487819} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b6c1d8-6172-501d-a590-456ba1e9c20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1bde4eb-16c8-5f7c-8125-caf4a9cb1949" :question/math-academy-id "q-45310" :question/problem "If $4c+5=3yc-2,$ then $c=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $c$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $5$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c+5 & =3yc-2 \\\\ 4c+5-5 & =3yc-2-5 \\\\ 4c & =3yc-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3yc$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c & =3yc-7 \\\\ 4c-3yc & =3yc-7-3yc \\\\ 4c-3yc & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $c$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $c$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $c$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c-3yc & =-7 \\\\ c(4-3y) & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4-3y)$ to isolate $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(4-3y) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{c(4-3y)}{4-3y} & =\\frac{-7}{4-3y} \\\\ \\frac{c\\cancel{(4-3y)}}{\\cancel{4-3y}} & =-\\frac{7}{4-3y} \\\\ c & =\\frac{7}{3y-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3y-4\\ne 0.$"}]}
